findings.body:
`AdaptersFactory.test.ts` covers `get` and `getProviders`, but nothing in the suite exercises the new `getGenericCalendarSuffixes()`. That method is the only place the per-provider map gets flattened, so a regression there (for example, dropping a provider's entries or returning the wrong list) would reach production with no test failure.

::code{file="packages/features/calendar-subscription/adapters/AdaptersFactory.ts" lines="61-71"}

A test that asserts the flattened result equals the four Google suffixes, and that Office 365 contributes nothing, would close the gap.

Several existing `findNextSubscriptionBatch` tests gained `AND: undefined` in their expected `where` objects. `toHaveBeenCalledWith` treats an `undefined` property the same as a missing one, so these lines add nothing to the assertion. They also obscure what the no-suffix path is supposed to produce. The check that matters is that no `AND` clause is added when the list is empty or missing, and that check is not being made explicitly.

::diff{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.test.ts" lines="131-136"}

The new test covers the populated path only. There is no case for an empty array (`genericCalendarSuffixes: []`) or for an omitted parameter, and those are the two branches of the ternary in the repository.

`getGenericCalendarSuffixes()` flattens every provider's list into one array, and the repository applies that array to every row in the batch, whatever its `integration`. The current lists only contain Google suffixes, so there is no bug today. But the per-provider map suggests the filter is meant to be scoped, and a future provider with a suffix that matches another provider's `externalId` would silently drop rows.

::code{file="packages/features/calendar-subscription/adapters/AdaptersFactory.ts" lines="67-70"}

The `NOT ... endsWith` predicates also cannot use an index, so each batch query scans with four extra predicates. With `take: 100` that is probably fine, but it's worth a look if this query runs often.

Two small readability points:

- `Object.keys(...).flatMap((provider) => GENERIC_CALENDAR_SUFFIXES[provider as CalendarSubscriptionProvider])` needs a cast to read the values. `Object.values(GENERIC_CALENDAR_SUFFIXES).flat()` says the same thing without a cast.
- In the repository, `genericCalendarSuffixes?.length` is checked and then `genericCalendarSuffixes?.map` is called. After the length check the optional chain is redundant.

::suggestion{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.ts" lines="41-45"}
AND: genericCalendarSuffixes?.length
  ? genericCalendarSuffixes.map((suffix) => ({
      NOT: { externalId: { endsWith: suffix } },
    }))
  : undefined,
